# Security

Security best practices for web scraping, LLM integration, and sensitive data handling.

## API Key Management

### Never Hardcode Keys
```typescript
// BAD - NEVER DO THIS
const apiKey = "sk-abc123...";

// GOOD - use environment variables
const apiKey = process.env.OPENAI_API_KEY;
```

### Key Storage
- **Development:** `.env.local` files (gitignored)
- **Production:** Platform secrets management (Vercel environment variables)
- **CI/CD:** GitHub Secrets or equivalent
- **Local testing:** Use test/dummy keys when possible

### Key Rotation
- Rotate keys every 90 days
- Use different keys per environment (dev, staging, prod)
- Immediately revoke compromised keys

## Rate Limiting & Polite Scraping

### Respect robots.txt
```typescript
import { RobotsParser } from "robots-parser";

async function canScrape(url: string): Promise<boolean> {
  const robotsUrl = new URL("/robots.txt", url).href;
  const res = await fetch(robotsUrl);
  const txt = await res.text();
  const robots = new RobotsParser(robotsUrl, txt);
  return robots.isAllowed(url, "TheTell-Bot/1.0");
}
```

### Rate Limiting
- Default: 1 request per second per domain
- Respect `Retry-After` headers
- Implement exponential backoff on 429/503

```typescript
class RateLimiter {
  private minInterval: number;
  private lastRequest = 0;

  constructor(requestsPerSecond: number = 1.0) {
    this.minInterval = 1000 / requestsPerSecond;
  }

  async wait(): Promise<void> {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = now - this.lastRequest;
    if (elapsed < this.minInterval) {
      await new Promise((resolve) =>
        setTimeout(resolve, this.minInterval - elapsed)
      );
    }
    this.lastRequest = Date.now();
  }
}
```

### User-Agent
- Identify your scraper with a descriptive User-Agent
- Include contact info for site owners

```typescript
const headers = {
  "User-Agent":
    "TheTell-Bot/1.0 (+https://thetell.example.com/bot; contact@example.com)",
};
```

## Input Validation

### Prevent Injection Attacks

#### SQL Injection
Use Prisma ORM — never construct raw SQL with string interpolation:

```typescript
// BAD - vulnerable to SQL injection
const result = await prisma.$queryRawUnsafe(
  `SELECT * FROM signals WHERE id = '${signalId}'`
);

// GOOD - Prisma client handles parameterization
const signal = await prisma.signal.findUnique({
  where: { id: signalId },
});

// GOOD - parameterized raw query if needed
const result = await prisma.$queryRaw`
  SELECT * FROM signals WHERE id = ${signalId}
`;
```

#### Command Injection
Never pass user input to shell commands. Use `fetch()` for HTTP requests:

```typescript
// BAD - command injection risk
import { execSync } from "child_process";
execSync(`curl ${userUrl}`);

// GOOD - use fetch for HTTP requests
const response = await fetch(userUrl);
```

#### XSS (Frontend)
- React escapes by default, but be careful with `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`
- Sanitize user-generated content with DOMPurify

```typescript
// BAD - XSS risk
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: userInput }} />

// GOOD - sanitize first
import DOMPurify from "dompurify";
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: DOMPurify.sanitize(userInput) }} />
```

### Validate All Inputs
Use Zod schemas for API route validation:

```typescript
import { z } from "zod";

const SignalCreateSchema = z.object({
  sourceUrl: z.string().url(), // validates URL format
  companyId: z.string().uuid(), // validates UUID format
  confidence: z.number().min(0).max(1), // range validation
});
```

## CORS Configuration

### Same-Origin by Default
Next.js API routes and server actions are same-origin by default. When deploying on Vercel, CORS is handled at the platform level. If you need to configure CORS for external API access, use middleware or route handler headers:

```typescript
// In a route handler, if cross-origin access is needed:
return NextResponse.json(data, {
  headers: {
    "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": process.env.FRONTEND_URL || "",
    "Access-Control-Allow-Methods": "GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE",
    "Access-Control-Allow-Headers": "Content-Type, Authorization",
  },
});
```

### Never Use `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` in Production
This allows any website to make requests to your API.

## Sensitive Data Handling

### PII (Personally Identifiable Information)
- **Never log PII** (names, emails, phone numbers)
- **Never send PII to LLMs** unless necessary and with consent
- **Anonymize** data when possible

```typescript
// BAD - logging PII
logger.info({ email: user.email }, "User requested signal");

// GOOD - anonymize
logger.info({ userId: user.id }, "User requested signal");
```

### Credentials & Tokens
- **Never commit** `.env.local`, credentials, or tokens
- **Never log** API keys, even partially
- **Use NextAuth** for session management — it handles httpOnly cookies automatically

```typescript
// BAD - storing token in localStorage (XSS risk)
localStorage.setItem("token", authToken);

// GOOD - NextAuth handles httpOnly session cookies automatically
// Session cookies are httpOnly, secure, and sameSite by default
import { auth } from "@/lib/auth";
const session = await auth();
```

### Database Credentials
- Use strong, unique passwords per environment
- Restrict database user permissions (principle of least privilege)
- Use connection pooling with authentication (Prisma handles this)

## HTTPS Enforcement

### Production
- **Vercel handles HTTPS automatically** — all deployments use HTTPS by default
- HSTS headers are configured at the platform level
- No code changes needed for HTTPS enforcement

### Development
- HTTP is acceptable for local development
- Document that production requires HTTPS (handled by Vercel)

## Dependency Security

### Keep Dependencies Updated
- Run `pnpm audit` regularly
- Use Dependabot or similar for automated updates

### Pin Dependencies
- Pin exact versions in production (`pnpm-lock.yaml`)
- Review major version upgrades before accepting

### Verify Package Sources
- Only install from official npm registry
- Check package popularity and maintenance status
- Review new dependencies before adding

## Security Checklist

Before deploying:
- [ ] No hardcoded secrets in code
- [ ] `.env.local` files are gitignored
- [ ] CORS restricted to specific origins
- [ ] HTTPS enforced in production (automatic on Vercel)
- [ ] Input validation on all API routes (Zod schemas)
- [ ] Rate limiting enabled for scraping
- [ ] Dependencies audited (`pnpm audit`)
- [ ] No PII in logs
- [ ] Database credentials are strong and unique
- [ ] Session tokens use httpOnly cookies (NextAuth default)
